.TH DEDUPE 1 "2026-08-29" "dedupe 1.5" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
dedupe \- remove duplicate lines without sorting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dedupe
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR spec ]
.RB [ \-U
.IR spec ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dedupe
writes each distinct input line to standard output exactly once, in
first-seen order.  With no
.I file
argument it reads standard input; the literal name
.B stdin
may be used among the file arguments.  Multiple files are treated as one
stream, so
.B dedupe
also merges.
.PP
Unlike
.BR sort (1)
.B \-u
it does not sort and does not need to hold the input in sorted form.  For
the single job of deduplicating a wordlist,
.BR rling (1)
is generally the better tool \(em it is faster and offers set subtraction
as well.
.B dedupe
is the smaller, dependency-light alternative.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-x
Store an xxHash digest of each line instead of the line itself.  Uses far
less memory on a large input.
.IP
This is a lossy trade.  Two distinct lines that hash to the same value are
treated as duplicates, and the second is silently discarded.  Do not use
.B \-x
when the output must be complete.
.TP
.B \-u
Decode
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
wrappers on output.
.TP
.BI \-S " spec"
Mark characters as requiring
.B $HEX[]
encoding on output.
.I spec
is a comma-separated list of literal characters, decimal values,
.BI 0x NN
hexadecimal values, and ranges such as
.BR a\-f ,
.B 0\-32
or
.BR 0x1a\-0x1f .
The resulting 256-entry map is printed to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-U " spec"
As
.BR \-S ,
but unmarks.
.TP
.B \-h, \-?
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage error.  Unreadable input files are
reported and skipped.
.SH EXAMPLES
Merge and deduplicate several lists:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dedupe leak1.txt leak2.txt leak3.txt > merged.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Deduplicate a list too large to hold in memory verbatim, accepting the
collision risk:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dedupe \-x enormous.txt > merged.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR rling (1),
.BR rehex (1),
.BR splitlen (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
